            <ArticleTitle>Decline in contraceptive use in India: A call for action.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13-16</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1877-5756(17)30182-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.srhc.2017.08.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Select estimates of contraceptive use in the 2015-16 National Family Health Survey (NFHS is equivalent to the Demographic and Health Survey in India) have come under the scrutiny of researchers and policy makers globally. Using four waves (1992-93, 1998-99, 2005-06, and 2015-16) of published NFHS reports, this article aims to highlight the change in Contraceptive Prevalence Rate (CPR) and initiate a discussion to help investigate the reasons for the recent decline of CPR.</AbstractText>
